## Context

The gaze review loop appears to conflate two decorrelation axes:
- ANGLE: which perspective reviews the work (correctness, consistency,
  scope, red team...) — seats differ by what they look at;
- LINEAGE: which model family reviews — seats differ by producer
  independence (the reason the judge panel excludes candidate families).
A third axis exists and is currently unexploited: PURE REPLICATION —
same model, same prompt, inter-run variation from temperature only.

## Scope

- Name the three axes in the review doctrine (rules/guards.md sibling
  or the review skills) and state what each buys and what it does not.
- Decide the house policy: how many angles, how many lineages, and when
  replication (temperature resampling) substitutes for a seat.
- The judge panel of the arena is a lineage-decorrelated SAME-ANGLE
  panel (one rubric, three families) — state that explicitly.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The three axes documented with what each controls
- [ ] gaze/review skills updated: angle vs lineage no longer conflated
- [ ] A decision rule: minimum decorrelation for a review to count as
      independent (incl. when replication suffices)
